  "query_preview": "..\n\nEast to southeast winds continue howling across Fort Peck Lake \nwith a Lake Wind Advisory set to expire at 4AM MDT Tuesday. Those\nwinds will relax during the day Tuesday as a wintry mix lifts \nnortheast across NE Montana. Ensembles continue to show a range of\nQPF between 0.10\" and 0.25\" for most, though little or no snow \naccumulation is expected save for nominal totals over higher \nterrain, near the Canadian border, and typical colder surfaces \nsuch as open fields and car tops.\n\nMidweek, a Pacific Northwest upper low will persist with preceding\nSouthwest flow aloft over NE Montana and surrounding regions.\nEventually as this trough translates eastward, precipitation\nchances will likely make a comeback late in the week or toward the\nupcoming weekend. However precipitation performs over the region,\nthis general pattern does also spell a continuation of below \nnormal temperatures through the week.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nNBM was cheered on for this forecast period capturing the colder\ntemperatures expected for the week as well as the wintry mix for\nTuesday and the renewed chances for precipitation late week.\nConfidence remains a bit below average on specific precipitation\namounts and precipitation type given marginal thermal profiles in\nplace. Only changes to the baseline involved visiting with near \nterm winds over the Fort Peck Lake area which continue to exceed \nexpectations and most guidance. Blending in a little higher \nresolution ensemble in with the NBM allowed for an upward\nadjustment followed by a nudge toward trends and observations.",
